      "question": {
        ":question/id": "9cc568a0-395c-59a2-8971-c56dbe75fd03",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the definite integral $\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3 x + 2}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =3 x + 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3 x + 2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3 x + 2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\ln(u)|_{5}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\ln(8)-\\ln(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\ln((2)^{3})-\\ln(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3\\ln(2)-\\ln(5)) \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\log{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3} + \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
