        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4 x + 7 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u & =1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4 x + 7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 5 \\\\ u & 11 & 27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{4 x + 7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{11}^{27}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{11}^{27}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{11}^{27}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\ln(u)|_{11}^{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\ln(27)-\\ln(11)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\ln((3)^{3})-\\ln(11)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\ln(3)-\\ln(11)) \\\\  & =- \\log{\\left(11 \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\log{\\left(33 \\right)}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
